# What never needs to reach a Vercel build.
#
# EVERY DIRECTORY ENTRY IS ANCHORED WITH A LEADING SLASH, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING.
# This file uses .gitignore syntax, where a bare name matches at ANY depth — so a
# bare `supabase` would also match utils/supabase/, which app/auth/confirm/route.ts
# and app/launch/page.tsx import. That would break `next build`, not just the
# Python bundle. Anchoring pins each entry to the repo root.
#
# WHETHER THIS FILE APPLIES TO GIT DEPLOYMENTS IS UNSETTLED. Vercel's own docs
# contradict each other — "only relevant when using Vercel CLI" (build features)
# vs. an unqualified description under .vercelignore. It is definitely honoured by
# `vercel build` / `vercel deploy` from a laptop. So nothing build-critical goes in
# here; the function bundle is trimmed by `excludeFiles` in vercel.json, which is
# documented and unambiguous. Confirm the git-deploy behaviour on a preview by
# opening /_src and checking whether docs/ and e2e/ are present.
#
# The concrete reason this file exists at all: .claude/worktrees is 4.8 GB of git
# worktrees. It is .gitignore'd, so a git deployment never sees it — but a local
# `vercel build` does not read .gitignore, and .claude is not on Vercel's default
# ignore list. Without this file, local verification globs 4.8 GB into the bundle,
# which is a large part of why the local CLI and the hosted builder have never
# agreed on function count or bundle size.

# --- Local-only build/test artefacts. Also .gitignore'd. ---
/.claude
/.agents
/.vscode
/coverage
/outputs
/test-results
/playwright-report
/playwright
/.pytest_cache
/api/.cache
/api/.pytest_cache
/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/.DS_Store

# --- Committed, but read by neither `next build` nor the Python function. ---
/api/tests          # 55 files; proven unreachable from api/index.py by import graph
/__tests__          # vitest only; already in tsconfig "exclude"
/e2e                # playwright only
/docs
/.github            # workflows run on GitHub, never on Vercel
/supabase           # migrations + seed; CLI and CI only. NOT utils/supabase.
/worker             # the desktop AI worker. See below — it must never deploy.
/api/evals          # measurement harnesses; run by hand, never imported by a route.

# NOT ignored, deliberately:
#   scripts/  — package.json's postinstall runs scripts/copy-scanner-wasm.mjs, which
#               copies the zxing WASM into public/ and throws if it fails.
#   public/   — app/(marketing)/privacy/page.tsx reads public/legal/privacy.html at
#               build time, and Hero.tsx serves /wireframe-scene.mp4 + /screenshots.

# --- /worker, and why it gets an entry in BOTH files ---
#
# The desktop AI worker is an outbound-only process that needs a GPU, a resident
# Ollama model and a long-lived loop. On Vercel it would have nothing to reach and
# a 60-second wall. It is not a thing that can deploy, and there is no arrangement
# in which it should.
#
# Merging it also made Vercel email "1 new project available to import — worker
# (python)". That is the Git integration's repo scan: `worker/requirements.txt` is
# a Python project manifest, and `worker/` was an unclaimed top-level directory.
# The offer is harmless, and accepting it would create a second billable project
# that could never work. Neither of these files suppresses that email — only a
# Vercel-side ignore setting does — but they are what stops the code shipping.
#
# excludeFiles IS CAPPED AT 256 CHARACTERS, and Vercel enforces it as a schema
# error that fails the deployment outright rather than a warning. That is not
# visible from vercel.json and there is no room to say it there, JSON having no
# comments — so it is recorded here. At the time of writing the value is 241, and
# the first attempt at this change blew the cap by adding api/pytest.ini and
# api/requirements-test.txt alongside the two entries that mattered. If a future
# entry does not fit, compact the list rather than dropping something: a bare
# `foo` and `foo/**` are not equivalent, and quietly losing node_modules/** would
# be far worse than a tight budget.
#
# The bundle half is the part that was actually wrong. `excludeFiles` in
# vercel.json works by ENUMERATION, so anything unlisted is included: worker/ and
# api/evals/ were both riding along inside every api/** function bundle. Nothing
# broke — Vercel installs the requirements.txt beside the function, so
# worker/requirements.txt was never read and pypdfium2 never reached the bundle —
# but it is dead weight, and it contradicts the entire reason worker/requirements.txt
# is a separate file from api/requirements.txt.
